{"scenario_text": "A population of mammalian cells grows in a nutrient-rich culture dish with limited surface area for attachment. Early on the cells proliferate freely, but as the dish fills, contact inhibition slows division until a maximum confluent density is reached. Researchers track the cell count over time to characterize how the growth rate depends on remaining available space.", "mechanism_tag": "contact-inhibited-proliferation", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the number of adherent cells in the culture", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 120.0], "target_state": "N", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Number of adherent cells in the culture dish", "initial_range": [1000.0, 50000.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["initial near-exponential growth", "saturation toward a carrying capacity", "monotonic increase"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded growth", "negative population", "oscillations"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A single-molecule enzyme converts substrate into product inside a cell lysate assay. At fixed enzyme concentration, the initial reaction velocity is measured across a range of substrate concentrations. As substrate is increased, the velocity rises steeply and then plateaus once the enzyme becomes saturated.", "mechanism_tag": "enzyme-substrate-saturation", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "v", "target_description": "Initial catalytic velocity of product formation", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Substrate concentration in the assay", "range": [0.1, 500.0]}, {"symbol": "E", "description": "Total enzyme concentration", "range": [0.01, 5.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["linear rise at low substrate", "plateau at high substrate", "proportional scaling with enzyme concentration"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["velocity decreasing with more substrate", "negative velocity", "unbounded increase"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A cell responds to a sudden step increase in extracellular ligand by activating a receptor-driven signaling kinase. The active kinase also promotes a slower negative feedback that deactivates it, producing a transient pulse of activity. Investigators measure how the active kinase level evolves after stimulation.", "mechanism_tag": "signaling-adaptation-pulse", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dK_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of active signaling kinase concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 60.0], "target_state": "K", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "K", "description": "Concentration of active signaling kinase", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.2]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Concentration of the slower feedback inhibitor", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.1]}], "expected_behaviors": ["transient rise then partial decline", "adaptation toward a lower plateau", "damped overshoot"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["sustained unbounded growth", "negative concentrations", "immediate steady state with no transient"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A membrane transporter moves a solute across the plasma membrane against its gradient. The uptake rate is measured as a function of external solute concentration at fixed transporter abundance. The rate increases with concentration but saturates as all transporters become occupied.", "mechanism_tag": "carrier-mediated-transport", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "J", "target_description": "Solute uptake flux across the membrane", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "C", "description": "External solute concentration", "range": [0.05, 200.0]}, {"symbol": "T", "description": "Relative transporter abundance in the membrane", "range": [0.1, 10.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["flux rises with external concentration", "saturating plateau", "flux increases with transporter abundance"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative flux", "linear unbounded growth", "flux independent of transporter abundance"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A cell degrades a specific protein after a synthesis burst is halted. The protein is removed by first-order proteolytic turnover, so its concentration decays over time. Biologists track the decline to infer the effective degradation dynamics.", "mechanism_tag": "protein-degradation-decay", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dP_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the target protein concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 100.0], "target_state": "P", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "P", "description": "Concentration of the target protein", "initial_range": [0.5, 10.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decay toward zero", "faster decline at higher concentration", "approach to a low baseline"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["increase over time", "negative concentration", "oscillations"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A cytoplasmic second messenger diffuses and is buffered as it spreads from a localized source at the membrane. At steady state, its concentration decreases with distance from the source due to combined diffusion and degradation. Researchers measure the concentration profile as a function of distance and degradation strength.", "mechanism_tag": "messenger-gradient-decay", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "M", "target_description": "Steady-state second-messenger concentration at a given distance from the source", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "x", "description": "Distance from the membrane source", "range": [0.0, 20.0]}, {"symbol": "k", "description": "Effective degradation rate constant", "range": [0.05, 2.0]}, {"symbol": "M0", "description": "Concentration at the source", "range": [1.0, 50.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["concentration decreases with distance", "steeper decline with stronger degradation", "scales with source concentration"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative concentration", "increase with distance", "concentration above source value"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A gene circuit exhibits self-activation, where a transcription factor promotes its own expression while being diluted by cell growth. Depending on parameters, the factor concentration can build up or relax. Modelers follow the factor abundance over time to study accumulation driven by cooperative self-activation and linear removal.", "mechanism_tag": "autoregulatory-gene-expression", "functional_family": "mixed", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dA_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the self-activating transcription factor concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 80.0], "target_state": "A", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "A", "description": "Concentration of the self-activating transcription factor", "initial_range": [0.0, 3.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["sigmoidal buildup from low state", "approach to a stable high plateau", "removal term balancing production"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded growth", "negative concentration", "sustained oscillations"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A cell membrane behaves as a capacitor that is charged by an injected transmembrane current while leaking through passive channels. When a constant current is applied, the membrane potential rises and relaxes toward a new steady value. Electrophysiologists record how the potential evolves after current onset.", "mechanism_tag": "membrane-potential-relaxation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dV_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the transmembrane potential", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 50.0], "target_state": "V", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "V", "description": "Transmembrane potential relative to resting value", "initial_range": [-20.0, 20.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic relaxation toward steady potential", "faster change when far from steady state", "bounded approach to equilibrium"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded drift", "oscillatory ringing", "instantaneous jump"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A dividing cell partitions its cytoplasmic volume between two daughters, and the time required to complete the division cycle depends on the initial cell size. Larger cells commit to division sooner, so cycle duration decreases with birth size following a scaling relationship. Cell biologists measure division time across a range of birth sizes and growth rates.", "mechanism_tag": "size-dependent-division-timing", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "Td", "target_description": "Time to complete the division cycle", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "Vb", "description": "Cell volume at birth", "range": [0.5, 5.0]}, {"symbol": "g", "description": "Specific volume growth rate", "range": [0.05, 1.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["division time decreases with birth size", "division time decreases with growth rate", "smooth monotonic scaling"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative division time", "increase with growth rate", "discontinuities"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A calcium-handling cell releases calcium from internal stores in response to a stimulus, with release amplified by calcium itself and then removed by pumps. Under sustained stimulation this produces repetitive spikes of cytosolic calcium. Researchers follow cytosolic and store calcium levels to capture the oscillatory release-and-refill cycle.", "mechanism_tag": "calcium-oscillation", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dC_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of cytosolic calcium concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 120.0], "target_state": "C", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "C", "description": "Cytosolic calcium concentration", "initial_range": [0.05, 0.5]}, {"symbol": "S", "description": "Calcium concentration in the internal store", "initial_range": [1.0, 5.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["repetitive spikes of cytosolic calcium", "store depletion and refill cycles", "sustained limit-cycle behavior"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative concentrations", "monotonic decay to zero with no spikes", "unbounded growth"]}, "id": "m2_cell_biology_and_signaling_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "cell_biology_and_signaling", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A neuron's membrane potential responds to injected current through the interplay of a fast regenerative sodium-like current and a slower recovery current. When driven above threshold, the membrane produces repetitive spiking, but below threshold it relaxes back to rest. We track the coupled evolution of the voltage and a lumped recovery variable under constant drive. The goal is to recover the voltage rate law from observed trajectories.", "mechanism_tag": "spiking-membrane-dynamics", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dv_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of membrane potential during excitable spiking dynamics.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 100.0], "target_state": "v", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "v", "description": "Membrane potential of the neuron.", "initial_range": [-80.0, -50.0]}, {"symbol": "w", "description": "Slow recovery current variable.", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.5]}], "expected_behaviors": ["limit-cycle oscillations above threshold", "relaxation to rest below threshold", "fast-slow separation of timescales"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded growth", "static equilibrium only"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a chemical synapse, the postsynaptic conductance rises rapidly following a presynaptic release event and then decays as bound neurotransmitter unbinds and is cleared from the cleft. Between events the conductance simply relaxes toward zero. We observe the conductance trajectory in response to a fixed release input at the start. The target is the conductance decay rate.", "mechanism_tag": "synaptic-conductance-decay", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dg_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of postsynaptic conductance during clearance.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 50.0], "target_state": "g", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "g", "description": "Postsynaptic conductance from bound neurotransmitter.", "initial_range": [0.5, 2.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decay toward zero", "single characteristic timescale"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["oscillation", "growth without input"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "The steady firing rate of a cortical neuron increases with the strength of its input current, but saturates once the input becomes large due to refractory limits on how fast the cell can fire. At low input the rate stays near zero until a threshold is crossed. We measure the equilibrium firing rate across independently applied constant input levels. The goal is to model the input-output rate relationship.", "mechanism_tag": "firing-rate-transfer", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "r", "target_description": "Steady-state firing rate of the neuron.", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "I", "description": "Constant injected input current.", "range": [0.0, 5.0]}, {"symbol": "r_max", "description": "Maximum achievable firing rate set by refractoriness.", "range": [50.0, 200.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["near-zero rate below threshold", "monotonic increase", "saturation at high input"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative rates", "unbounded increase"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A population of interconnected excitatory neurons shows self-limiting growth of average activity: activity recruits more firing through recurrent excitation, but rises are curbed as the pool of available neurons and resources becomes limited. Starting from a small perturbation, activity climbs and settles at a stable plateau. We record the mean population activity over time. The aim is to recover the activity growth rate law.", "mechanism_tag": "recurrent-population-activity", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dA_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of mean population activity under recurrent excitation.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 40.0], "target_state": "A", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "A", "description": "Mean activity level of the excitatory population.", "initial_range": [0.01, 0.2]}], "expected_behaviors": ["initial accelerating growth", "saturation to stable plateau"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded growth", "sustained oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "During repeated presynaptic stimulation, a synapse depresses because its pool of readily releasable vesicles is depleted faster than it can be replenished. The available resource fraction falls when release occurs and recovers slowly between events. We track the readily releasable resource fraction under a constant release drive. The target is the resource depletion-recovery rate.", "mechanism_tag": "short-term-depression", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dR_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the readily releasable vesicle resource fraction.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 30.0], "target_state": "R", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "R", "description": "Fraction of readily releasable synaptic resources.", "initial_range": [0.3, 1.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["depletion under drive", "recovery toward full pool", "approach to steady balance"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["resource exceeding one", "negative resource"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "The passive spread of voltage along a thin dendritic cable attenuates with distance from the injection site because of leak across the membrane. At steady state, the potential falls off smoothly as one moves away from the current source. We measure the steady membrane potential as a function of distance along the dendrite and the membrane leak strength. The goal is to model spatial voltage attenuation.", "mechanism_tag": "dendritic-voltage-attenuation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "V", "target_description": "Steady-state membrane potential at a given distance along a passive dendrite.", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "x", "description": "Distance from the current injection site along the dendrite.", "range": [0.0, 5.0]}, {"symbol": "lambda", "description": "Characteristic length constant of the cable.", "range": [0.3, 2.0]}, {"symbol": "V0", "description": "Membrane potential at the injection site.", "range": [5.0, 40.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decay with distance", "faster decay for shorter length constant"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["increase with distance", "negative potential relative to rest"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Two mutually inhibitory neural pools form a competition circuit that alternates dominance, as in perceptual rivalry. Each pool suppresses the other, and slow adaptation of the winning pool eventually lets the loser take over, producing alternating activity. We follow both pool activities plus an adaptation variable from initial conditions. The aim is to recover the activity rate law of one pool.", "mechanism_tag": "mutual-inhibition-rivalry", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dx_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the first pool's activity under mutual inhibition and adaptation.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 100.0], "target_state": "x", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "x", "description": "Activity of the first competing neural pool.", "initial_range": [0.0, 1.0]}, {"symbol": "y", "description": "Activity of the second competing neural pool.", "initial_range": [0.0, 1.0]}, {"symbol": "a", "description": "Slow adaptation variable of the first pool.", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.5]}], "expected_behaviors": ["alternating dominance", "slow adaptation-driven switching", "bounded activities"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["fixed steady state only", "unbounded activity"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "The activation of a voltage-gated ion channel population is described by the fraction of channels in the open state, which relaxes toward a voltage-dependent steady value with a voltage-dependent time constant. Under a voltage step held fixed, the open fraction evolves smoothly to its new target. We observe the gating variable's trajectory. The goal is to recover its rate of change.", "mechanism_tag": "channel-gating-relaxation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dm_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the channel open-state fraction toward its voltage-dependent target.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 20.0], "target_state": "m", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "m", "description": "Fraction of voltage-gated channels in the open state.", "initial_range": [0.0, 1.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic relaxation to steady value", "bounded between zero and one"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["overshoot beyond bounds", "oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Intracellular calcium concentration in a neuron rises with influx during activity and is removed by buffering and pumping that act more strongly at higher concentrations. Following an influx event, calcium relaxes back toward its low resting level. We track the free calcium concentration over time under a fixed initial load. The target is the calcium concentration rate of change.", "mechanism_tag": "calcium-clearance", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dCa_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of free intracellular calcium concentration during clearance.", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 60.0], "target_state": "Ca", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "Ca", "description": "Free intracellular calcium concentration.", "initial_range": [0.5, 5.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["decay toward resting level", "concentration-dependent removal rate"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative concentration", "growth without influx"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "The equilibrium transmembrane potential at which no net flow of a single ion species occurs depends on the ratio of its concentrations across the membrane and the absolute temperature. At balance, the electrical driving force offsets the chemical concentration gradient. We evaluate this reversal potential across independently sampled concentration ratios and temperatures. The goal is to model the equilibrium potential.", "mechanism_tag": "ionic-reversal-potential", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "E", "target_description": "Equilibrium reversal potential for a single ion species.", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "ratio", "description": "Ratio of extracellular to intracellular ion concentration.", "range": [0.1, 30.0]}, {"symbol": "T", "description": "Absolute temperature.", "range": [280.0, 315.0]}, {"symbol": "z", "description": "Ionic valence of the species.", "range": [1.0, 2.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["logarithmic dependence on concentration ratio", "sign change as ratio crosses one", "scaling with temperature"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["divergence at finite ratio", "time dependence"]}, "id": "m2_neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "neuroscience_and_neural_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A purified enzyme is assayed at fixed enzyme concentration across a range of substrate concentrations under initial-rate conditions. The measured initial reaction velocity rises steeply at low substrate and then plateaus as the active sites become fully occupied. Temperature, pH, and enzyme amount are held constant so that only substrate availability modulates the response. The goal is to recover the instantaneous velocity as a function of substrate concentration.", "mechanism_tag": "single-substrate-saturation", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "v", "target_description": "Initial catalytic velocity of the enzyme", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Substrate concentration", "range": [0.01, 100.0]}, {"symbol": "Vmax", "description": "Maximal attainable velocity", "range": [1.0, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "Km", "description": "Substrate concentration at half-maximal velocity", "range": [0.5, 20.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic increase with substrate", "approaches a finite plateau at high substrate", "near-linear at very low substrate"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded growth", "negative velocity", "oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "An enzyme catalyzes a reaction whose rate is suppressed when an inhibitor competes for the same binding pocket as the substrate. Initial velocities are recorded at fixed inhibitor concentration while substrate is varied. The apparent affinity for substrate weakens as inhibitor rises, but the maximal velocity remains reachable at sufficiently high substrate. The task is to express instantaneous velocity in terms of substrate and inhibitor levels.", "mechanism_tag": "competitive-inhibition", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "v", "target_description": "Initial catalytic velocity in the presence of a competing inhibitor", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Substrate concentration", "range": [0.05, 100.0]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Inhibitor concentration", "range": [0.0, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "Ki", "description": "Inhibitor dissociation constant", "range": [0.5, 25.0]}, {"symbol": "Km", "description": "Intrinsic substrate half-saturation constant", "range": [0.5, 20.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["velocity decreases as inhibitor increases at fixed substrate", "high substrate overcomes inhibition", "saturating in substrate"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["velocity increasing with inhibitor", "negative velocity", "unbounded growth"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A multimeric enzyme with several interacting subunits shows a sigmoidal dependence of activity on substrate because substrate binding to one site enhances binding at others. At low substrate the response is shallow, then it rises sharply through a transition region before leveling off. Cooperativity strength governs the steepness of this switch-like transition. The objective is to describe fractional activity as a function of substrate concentration.", "mechanism_tag": "cooperative-binding", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "theta", "target_description": "Fractional saturation of the cooperative enzyme's binding sites", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Substrate concentration", "range": [0.01, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "K", "description": "Apparent half-saturation concentration", "range": [0.5, 20.0]}, {"symbol": "n", "description": "Cooperativity coefficient", "range": [1.0, 4.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["sigmoidal rise with substrate", "bounded between zero and one", "steeper transition for higher cooperativity"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["exceeding unity", "negative saturation", "linear response"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A ligand binds reversibly to a receptor in a well-mixed solution starting from an initial pool of free ligand and empty binding sites. Over time the bound complex accumulates through association and is depleted through dissociation until equilibrium is reached. The concentrations of free ligand and complex evolve jointly from their starting values. The interest is in how the bound-complex concentration changes over time.", "mechanism_tag": "reversible-ligand-binding-relaxation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dC_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the ligand-receptor complex concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 20.0], "target_state": "C", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "C", "description": "Bound ligand-receptor complex concentration", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.5]}, {"symbol": "L", "description": "Free ligand concentration", "initial_range": [1.0, 10.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic approach to equilibrium", "complex accumulation slows as sites fill", "relaxation toward a steady state"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["sustained oscillation", "negative concentrations", "unbounded growth"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a batch bioreactor a fixed amount of enzyme converts substrate into product, with substrate being consumed as the reaction proceeds. As substrate declines the reaction slows because the active sites are no longer saturated, producing a characteristic progress curve. No new substrate is added and product does not inhibit the enzyme. The goal is to track how the remaining substrate concentration evolves over time.", "mechanism_tag": "batch-substrate-depletion", "functional_family": "mixed", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dS_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of substrate concentration during turnover", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 100.0], "target_state": "S", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Remaining substrate concentration", "initial_range": [1.0, 50.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decrease of substrate", "near-linear consumption when saturated", "slowing consumption as substrate becomes scarce"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["substrate increasing", "negative concentration", "oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "An enzyme loses catalytic activity when incubated at elevated temperature because the folded protein irreversibly unfolds and aggregates. The concentration of active enzyme decays as inactive forms accumulate, following a rate that depends on the incubation temperature. Substrate is present in excess so activity directly reflects the active enzyme pool. The task is to describe how the active enzyme concentration falls over time at fixed temperature.", "mechanism_tag": "thermal-inactivation-decay", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dE_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of active enzyme concentration during thermal inactivation", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 60.0], "target_state": "E", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "E", "description": "Concentration of catalytically active enzyme", "initial_range": [0.5, 10.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decay of active enzyme", "approach to zero activity", "faster decay at higher effective rate"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["reactivation", "negative concentration", "growth of active enzyme"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "An enzyme's turnover depends on the protonation state of catalytic residues, so its activity varies with the pH of the buffer. Activity is low at extreme acidic and basic conditions and peaks near an optimal pH where the residues are ideally charged. All other conditions including substrate concentration are held saturating. The objective is to express the relative activity as a function of buffer pH.", "mechanism_tag": "ph-dependent-activity", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "a", "target_description": "Relative catalytic activity as a function of pH", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "H", "description": "Hydrogen ion concentration in the buffer", "range": [1e-09, 0.0001]}, {"symbol": "Ka1", "description": "Acid dissociation constant of the acidic catalytic group", "range": [1e-08, 1e-05]}, {"symbol": "Ka2", "description": "Acid dissociation constant of the basic catalytic group", "range": [1e-07, 0.0001]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["bell-shaped dependence on pH", "activity declines at extreme proton concentrations", "single interior maximum"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["monotonic increase over full range", "negative activity", "multiple maxima"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A metabolic pathway contains a feedback loop where the end product represses the activity of the first committed enzyme, while the product itself is consumed downstream. Under continuous substrate supply this arrangement can drive sustained oscillations in the intermediate and product concentrations. The delay introduced by the feedback allows overshoot and recovery cycles. The interest is in how the product concentration evolves over time.", "mechanism_tag": "feedback-metabolic-oscillation", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dP_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the pathway end-product concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 80.0], "target_state": "P", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "P", "description": "End-product concentration exerting feedback repression", "initial_range": [0.1, 2.0]}, {"symbol": "M", "description": "Upstream metabolic intermediate concentration", "initial_range": [0.1, 2.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["sustained or damped oscillations", "phase lag between intermediate and product", "bounded concentrations"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded growth", "negative concentrations", "immediate monotonic settling with no overshoot"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "An enzyme requires two substrates that bind sequentially before catalysis, and the initial velocity depends jointly on both substrate concentrations. Raising either substrate increases velocity toward a shared maximal rate, but low levels of either limit turnover. Enzyme concentration and environmental conditions are fixed. The objective is to recover initial velocity as a function of the two substrate concentrations.", "mechanism_tag": "two-substrate-sequential", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "v", "target_description": "Initial velocity of a two-substrate sequential reaction", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "A", "description": "Concentration of first substrate", "range": [0.05, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "B", "description": "Concentration of second substrate", "range": [0.05, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "Vmax", "description": "Maximal velocity when both substrates saturate", "range": [1.0, 40.0]}, {"symbol": "Ka", "description": "Effective half-saturation constant for the substrates", "range": [0.5, 15.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["velocity increases with either substrate", "saturates when both substrates are high", "low velocity when either substrate is scarce"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative velocity", "unbounded growth", "activity independent of one substrate"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "An enzyme is inhibited by high concentrations of its own substrate, which binds a second nonproductive site once the primary site is occupied. As substrate rises the velocity first increases, reaches a peak, and then declines because more enzyme is trapped in the unproductive state. All other conditions are held constant. The task is to describe initial velocity across the full substrate range.", "mechanism_tag": "substrate-inhibition", "functional_family": "mixed", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "v", "target_description": "Initial velocity subject to inhibition by excess substrate", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Substrate concentration", "range": [0.01, 200.0]}, {"symbol": "Vmax", "description": "Maximal velocity parameter", "range": [1.0, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "Km", "description": "Primary-site half-saturation constant", "range": [0.5, 15.0]}, {"symbol": "Ksi", "description": "Substrate inhibition constant for the secondary site", "range": [10.0, 200.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["rise to an interior peak velocity", "decline at very high substrate", "near-linear rise at low substrate"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["monotonic saturation with no decline", "negative velocity", "unbounded growth"]}, "id": "m2_enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "enzyme_kinetics_and_biochemistry", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A population of microorganisms grows in a nutrient-rich chemostat where resources are effectively unlimited over the observation window. The per-capita growth rate remains constant, and the abundance increases without any density-dependent limitation. Researchers track the total cell count through time from a small inoculum. The goal is to recover how the population accumulates under unrestricted proliferation.", "mechanism_tag": "unlimited-proliferation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of population abundance under unlimited resources", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 20.0], "target_state": "N", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Population abundance of the microorganism", "initial_range": [1.0, 10.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic increase", "accelerating growth over time"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["saturation to a fixed ceiling", "decline toward zero"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A grazing herbivore population inhabits a pasture with finite carrying capacity determined by fixed vegetation availability. Growth is rapid when the population is small but slows as crowding intensifies and resources per individual dwindle. Field counts are recorded from an initial size well below capacity. The objective is to capture the self-limiting approach to a stable abundance.", "mechanism_tag": "density-limited-growth", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of herbivore abundance under crowding-limited growth", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 30.0], "target_state": "N", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Herbivore population abundance", "initial_range": [5.0, 50.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["sigmoid approach to a stable plateau", "slowing growth near carrying capacity"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded exponential increase", "sustained oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a predator-prey system, a prey species and its specialist predator interact within an enclosed reserve with stable abiotic conditions. Prey reproduce in the predator's absence while predators decline without prey, and encounters couple the two abundances. Ecologists monitor both populations through many cycles. The task is to reconstruct the coupled dynamics that produce recurring boom-and-bust patterns.", "mechanism_tag": "predator-prey-cycles", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of prey abundance in a coupled predator-prey interaction", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 50.0], "target_state": "N", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Prey population abundance", "initial_range": [10.0, 100.0]}, {"symbol": "P", "description": "Predator population abundance", "initial_range": [5.0, 40.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["sustained cyclic fluctuations", "phase lag between predator and prey peaks"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["monotonic convergence to a single fixed point", "runaway divergence of both populations"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A predator consumes prey whose availability varies across experimental enclosures. At low prey density the predator's intake rises steeply, but handling and processing time cause intake to level off as prey become abundant. Each enclosure provides an independent measurement of per-predator consumption at a fixed prey density. The aim is to describe how individual feeding rate depends on prey density.", "mechanism_tag": "saturating-functional-response", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "f", "target_description": "Per-predator prey consumption rate at a given prey density", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "R", "description": "Prey density available to the predator", "range": [0.1, 200.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["increase with prey density", "saturation toward a maximum intake rate"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded linear growth without limit", "decline at high prey density"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Two competing plant species share a limited common habitat, each reducing the other's effective growth through resource competition. Their combined dynamics are shaped by intrinsic growth, self-crowding, and mutual suppression. Observers follow both abundances from various starting mixtures until settling. The goal is to recover the coupled competitive dynamics governing coexistence or exclusion.", "mechanism_tag": "interspecific-competition", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN1_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the first competitor's abundance under mutual competition", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 40.0], "target_state": "N1", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N1", "description": "Abundance of the first competing species", "initial_range": [5.0, 80.0]}, {"symbol": "N2", "description": "Abundance of the second competing species", "initial_range": [5.0, 80.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["convergence to a stable community state", "suppression of growth by the competitor"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["perpetual oscillation", "unbounded growth of either species"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A sparse sexually reproducing animal population suffers reduced per-capita growth when abundance is very low due to difficulty finding mates. Above a critical threshold density the population grows, but below it the population declines toward extinction. Managers track abundance from different starting sizes. The objective is to capture the low-density growth penalty that creates a tipping point.", "mechanism_tag": "mate-limitation-threshold", "functional_family": "mixed", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of abundance with reduced growth at low density", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 40.0], "target_state": "N", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Population abundance of the reproducing species", "initial_range": [1.0, 100.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["decline below a critical threshold", "growth toward a ceiling above the threshold"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["growth from arbitrarily small abundance", "single globally attracting nonzero state regardless of start"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a large well-mixed metapopulation of habitat patches, patches switch between occupied and empty states. Occupied patches send colonists that settle empty patches, while occupied patches also go locally extinct. Surveys record the fraction of patches occupied over time. The aim is to reconstruct how patch occupancy evolves under colonization and extinction balance.", "mechanism_tag": "patch-occupancy-dynamics", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dp_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the fraction of occupied habitat patches", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 50.0], "target_state": "p", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "p", "description": "Fraction of habitat patches currently occupied", "initial_range": [0.01, 0.9]}], "expected_behaviors": ["approach to a stable occupancy fraction", "faster colonization at intermediate occupancy"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["occupancy exceeding one", "unbounded exponential increase"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Across many isolated islands of differing area, the number of resident species at ecological equilibrium is measured. Larger islands consistently support more species, but the gain diminishes proportionally as area increases. Each island contributes one independent equilibrium measurement. The goal is to describe how equilibrium species number scales with island area.", "mechanism_tag": "area-richness-scaling", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "S", "target_description": "Equilibrium number of resident species on an island", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "A", "description": "Island area", "range": [0.1, 10000.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["increase with area", "diminishing proportional gain at larger area"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["decrease with area", "saturation to a hard constant ceiling"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A univoltine insect population is censused once per generation, where crowding within a generation strongly depresses the number of survivors reproducing in the next. At low density nearly all reproduce, but overcompensating crowding sharply reduces recruitment at high density. Ecologists collect paired successive-generation abundance measurements across many densities. The target is the mapping from one generation's abundance to the next generation's expected abundance.", "mechanism_tag": "overcompensating-recruitment", "functional_family": "mixed", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "N_next", "target_description": "Expected next-generation abundance given current-generation abundance", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Current-generation adult abundance", "range": [0.1, 500.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["rise then fall as density increases", "peak recruitment at intermediate density"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["monotonic saturation without a peak", "negative next-generation abundance"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A juvenile cohort experiences density-dependent mortality between recruitment and maturity within a single season with stable environmental conditions. Starting from an initial cohort size, individuals are lost at a rate that intensifies with current cohort density due to competition for refuges. The surviving number is tracked continuously through the developmental window. The objective is to recover how cohort abundance depletes under crowding-driven loss.", "mechanism_tag": "density-dependent-mortality", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dN_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of surviving cohort abundance under density-dependent loss", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 15.0], "target_state": "N", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "N", "description": "Surviving juvenile cohort abundance", "initial_range": [50.0, 1000.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decline over time", "faster proportional loss at higher density"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["increase in abundance", "abundance going below zero"]}, "id": "m2_population_ecology_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "population_ecology", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A novel respiratory pathogen spreads through a fully susceptible population where recovered individuals gain lasting immunity. Public health officials track how the number of currently infectious individuals rises, peaks, and declines as the susceptible pool is depleted. The interplay between new infections and recovery governs the outbreak trajectory. No births, deaths, or waning immunity occur over the study window.", "mechanism_tag": "susceptible-infectious-recovery-transmission", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dI_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the infectious population over time", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 120.0], "target_state": "I", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Number of susceptible individuals", "initial_range": [900.0, 1000.0]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Number of infectious individuals", "initial_range": [1.0, 20.0]}, {"symbol": "R", "description": "Number of recovered immune individuals", "initial_range": [0.0, 5.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["single epidemic peak in infectious count", "monotonic depletion of susceptibles", "final size below total population"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative compartment values", "sustained oscillations", "unbounded growth"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a region with an endemic disease that confers no permanent immunity, individuals cycle between susceptible and infectious states, and demographic turnover continually replenishes susceptibles. Epidemiologists observe that the infection settles toward a persistent nonzero level rather than dying out. Constant birth and death rates maintain the population near a fixed size. The balance between reinfection, recovery back to susceptibility, and turnover shapes the endemic equilibrium.", "mechanism_tag": "endemic-reinfection-turnover", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dI_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the infectious fraction over time", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 300.0], "target_state": "I", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Fraction of the population that is susceptible", "initial_range": [0.5, 0.95]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Fraction of the population that is infectious", "initial_range": [0.01, 0.2]}], "expected_behaviors": ["approach to stable endemic level", "damped transient before equilibrium", "bounded fractions between zero and one"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["divergence to infinity", "negative fractions", "permanent extinction when transmission is high"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A vector-borne infection is studied where a pathogen circulates between a human host population and a mosquito vector population. The infectious prevalence in humans depends on bites from infected mosquitoes, while mosquito infection depends on biting infected humans. Investigators want to describe how coupled prevalence in both populations evolves toward a shared steady state. Vector abundance and biting rate are held fixed as background conditions.", "mechanism_tag": "host-vector-cross-infection", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dIh_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of infectious human fraction over time", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 200.0], "target_state": "Ih", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "Ih", "description": "Fraction of humans that are infectious", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.1]}, {"symbol": "Iv", "description": "Fraction of vectors that are infectious", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.1]}], "expected_behaviors": ["coupled rise toward joint equilibrium", "cross-dependence between the two prevalences", "bounded prevalences"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative prevalence", "unbounded growth", "independent evolution of the two states"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A chronic noninfectious condition progresses in stages within a monitored cohort, with individuals advancing from an early asymptomatic stage to a symptomatic stage and eventually to an end-stage. Clinicians track how the number of people in the symptomatic stage changes as inflow from early stage and outflow to end-stage occur. Progression rates between consecutive stages drive the dynamics. Recruitment into the earliest stage is held constant.", "mechanism_tag": "staged-disease-progression", "functional_family": "mixed", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dM_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the number in the symptomatic middle stage over time", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 60.0], "target_state": "M", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "E", "description": "Number in the early asymptomatic stage", "initial_range": [100.0, 500.0]}, {"symbol": "M", "description": "Number in the symptomatic stage", "initial_range": [0.0, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "L", "description": "Number in the end-stage", "initial_range": [0.0, 20.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["transient rise then decline in middle stage", "monotonic accumulation in end-stage", "sequential flow through stages"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["backward flow to earlier stages", "negative counts", "oscillatory staging"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "During an outbreak, the effective per-contact transmission is reduced by behavioral interventions whose intensity depends on the currently observed infection level. Researchers measure the instantaneous force of infection as a function of contact rate, current prevalence, and the strength of behavioral response. This is treated as an algebraic response under varying sampled conditions rather than a time integration. Population size and susceptibility are fixed background quantities.", "mechanism_tag": "behavior-modulated-force-of-infection", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "lambda", "target_description": "Instantaneous force of infection experienced by susceptibles", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "c", "description": "Average contact rate per individual", "range": [1.0, 30.0]}, {"symbol": "p", "description": "Current infectious prevalence fraction", "range": [0.0, 0.5]}, {"symbol": "b", "description": "Behavioral response strength reducing effective transmission", "range": [0.0, 5.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["increase with contact rate and prevalence", "suppression as behavioral response grows", "saturating dependence on response strength"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative force of infection", "growth with increasing behavioral response", "time-dependent output"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A seasonally forced infection shows recurrent yearly waves driven by periodic variation in transmissibility tied to environmental cycles. Epidemiologists observe repeated epidemic peaks in the infectious population that neither die out nor grow without bound. The periodic modulation of transmission combined with recovery and susceptible replenishment sustains the oscillations. The amplitude and period of environmental forcing are fixed inputs to the dynamics.", "mechanism_tag": "seasonally-forced-recurrence", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dI_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the infectious fraction under seasonal forcing", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 1000.0], "target_state": "I", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Fraction of the population that is susceptible", "initial_range": [0.3, 0.9]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Fraction of the population that is infectious", "initial_range": [0.001, 0.05]}], "expected_behaviors": ["recurrent annual epidemic peaks", "bounded sustained oscillation", "phase locking to seasonal cycle"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["monotonic approach to a fixed point only", "unbounded amplitude growth", "negative fractions"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "An accumulating exposure disease relates individual risk of developing illness to the total cumulative dose of a harmful agent received. In a cross-sectional analysis, the probability of disease onset is modeled as an instantaneous function of cumulative exposure, exposure duration, and individual susceptibility. Investigators sample many individuals with different exposure histories. Genetic and treatment factors are held fixed as background.", "mechanism_tag": "cumulative-exposure-risk", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "P", "target_description": "Probability of disease onset given accumulated exposure", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "D", "description": "Cumulative exposure dose", "range": [0.0, 100.0]}, {"symbol": "T", "description": "Duration of exposure in years", "range": [0.1, 50.0]}, {"symbol": "s", "description": "Individual susceptibility coefficient", "range": [0.1, 3.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["probability increases with dose and duration", "monotonic increase with susceptibility", "output bounded between zero and one"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["probability above one or below zero", "decrease with increasing dose", "time integration"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A vaccination campaign moves individuals from the susceptible pool into a protected class while an infection circulates. Modelers examine how the depletion of susceptibles through both natural infection and vaccination determines whether the outbreak is contained. The vaccination rate acts alongside transmission and recovery. Population turnover is negligible over the campaign period.", "mechanism_tag": "vaccination-susceptible-depletion", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dS_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the susceptible population under vaccination and infection", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 90.0], "target_state": "S", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "S", "description": "Number of susceptible individuals", "initial_range": [800.0, 1000.0]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Number of infectious individuals", "initial_range": [1.0, 30.0]}, {"symbol": "V", "description": "Number of vaccinated protected individuals", "initial_range": [0.0, 50.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decline of susceptibles", "accelerated depletion under vaccination", "outbreak suppression at high vaccination"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["increase in susceptibles without births", "negative counts", "protected individuals returning to susceptible"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Contact tracing and isolation remove detected infectious individuals from circulation at a rate depending on surveillance intensity. Analysts measure the instantaneous net reproduction number as a function of baseline transmissibility, isolation efficiency, and detection delay. This is evaluated as an algebraic control outcome across sampled program settings. Underlying contact structure and recovery rate are fixed background conditions.", "mechanism_tag": "isolation-controlled-reproduction", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "Reff", "target_description": "Effective net reproduction number under isolation and tracing", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "R0", "description": "Baseline reproduction number without control", "range": [1.0, 8.0]}, {"symbol": "q", "description": "Fraction of infectious cases detected and isolated", "range": [0.0, 1.0]}, {"symbol": "d", "description": "Mean detection delay in days", "range": [0.0, 14.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["reduction with higher detection fraction", "increase with longer detection delay", "scaling with baseline reproduction number"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative reproduction number", "increase with better detection", "time evolution"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A disease with partial cross-immunity involves two co-circulating strains competing for a shared host population, where infection by one strain confers reduced susceptibility to the other. Epidemiologists follow how the infectious prevalence of each strain evolves through competitive suppression. The coupling arises from immune interaction between strains. Total population size and recovery rates are held constant.", "mechanism_tag": "two-strain-cross-immunity-competition", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dI1_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of infectious prevalence for strain one over time", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 250.0], "target_state": "I1", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "I1", "description": "Infectious prevalence fraction for strain one", "initial_range": [0.001, 0.1]}, {"symbol": "I2", "description": "Infectious prevalence fraction for strain two", "initial_range": [0.001, 0.1]}], "expected_behaviors": ["competitive suppression between strains", "possible dominance of one strain or coexistence", "bounded prevalences"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative prevalence", "unbounded growth of either strain", "independent uncoupled dynamics"]}, "id": "m2_epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "epidemiology_and_disease_dynamics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a large randomly mating population, a single locus has two alleles under selection where each genotype has a distinct relative fitness. Starting from an initial allele frequency, the frequency of the favored allele changes generation after generation due to differential survival and reproduction. Researchers track the trajectory of the allele frequency over many generations under constant selection coefficients. Mutation and migration are assumed negligible over the observation window.", "mechanism_tag": "directional-selection-allele-frequency", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dp_dt", "target_description": "rate of change of the favored allele frequency per generation", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 200.0], "target_state": "p", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "p", "description": "frequency of the favored allele", "initial_range": [0.01, 0.2]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic increase toward fixation", "S-shaped approach to an upper bound"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["frequency exceeding one or below zero", "oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A small isolated population experiences random sampling of gametes each generation, causing allele frequencies to drift without any selective pressure. The variance in allele frequency among many independent replicate populations grows as generations pass. Investigators measure how the expected heterozygosity within populations declines over time due to this stochastic sampling. The effective population size remains fixed throughout.", "mechanism_tag": "drift-heterozygosity-decay", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dH_dt", "target_description": "rate of change of average heterozygosity per generation", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 100.0], "target_state": "H", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "H", "description": "expected heterozygosity within the population", "initial_range": [0.2, 0.5]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decay toward zero", "decreasing rate as heterozygosity shrinks"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["increase over time", "negative heterozygosity"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Two loci on the same chromosome begin in strong statistical association after a population admixture event. Each generation, recombination between the loci breaks down the association at a rate governed by the recombination fraction. Geneticists follow the decline of the association measure across successive generations in a randomly mating population. No selection acts on either locus.", "mechanism_tag": "linkage-disequilibrium-decay", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dD_dt", "target_description": "rate of change of the linkage association measure per generation", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 50.0], "target_state": "D", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "D", "description": "gametic association measure between two loci", "initial_range": [0.05, 0.25]}], "expected_behaviors": ["geometric decay toward zero", "faster decay for larger recombination fraction"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["growth in association", "sign reversal"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a population at reproductive equilibrium, the three genotype proportions at a single locus depend only on the underlying allele frequency under random mating. Given a specified frequency of one allele, the expected proportion of heterozygous individuals follows directly from the mating scheme. Researchers sample many populations at different allele frequencies to record heterozygote proportions. Non-random mating and selection are absent.", "mechanism_tag": "random-mating-heterozygote-proportion", "functional_family": "polynomial", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "H_freq", "target_description": "expected proportion of heterozygous individuals at the locus", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "p", "description": "frequency of the reference allele", "range": [0.0, 1.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["maximum at intermediate allele frequency", "symmetric response around one-half"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["heterozygote proportion above one", "negative values"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A recurrent deleterious mutation continually introduces a harmful allele that selection removes each generation, producing a balance. The equilibrium frequency of the harmful allele reflects the tension between the mutation rate supplying it and the selection strength eliminating it. Investigators sample populations spanning a range of mutation rates and selection coefficients to record the standing equilibrium frequency. The population is large and randomly mating.", "mechanism_tag": "mutation-selection-balance", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "q_eq", "target_description": "equilibrium frequency of the deleterious allele", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "u", "description": "mutation rate producing the deleterious allele", "range": [1e-06, 0.0001]}, {"symbol": "s", "description": "selection coefficient against the deleterious homozygote", "range": [0.01, 0.5]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["equilibrium rises with mutation rate", "equilibrium falls with stronger selection"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["frequency exceeding one", "negative equilibrium"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "Two partially isolated subpopulations exchange migrants each generation while one allele differs in frequency between them. Migration progressively homogenizes the allele frequency in the recipient population toward the source frequency. Researchers track the allele frequency in the recipient population over successive generations at a fixed migration rate. Selection and drift are treated as negligible.", "mechanism_tag": "migration-frequency-homogenization", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dq_dt", "target_description": "rate of change of recipient allele frequency per generation", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 60.0], "target_state": "q", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "q", "description": "allele frequency in the recipient subpopulation", "initial_range": [0.0, 0.4]}], "expected_behaviors": ["asymptotic approach to source frequency", "monotonic convergence"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["overshoot beyond source frequency", "oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "At a single locus the heterozygote has higher fitness than either homozygote, maintaining both alleles in the population. Starting from various initial frequencies, the allele frequency moves toward a stable polymorphic equilibrium determined by the two homozygote selection coefficients. Geneticists follow the frequency trajectory across generations in a large randomly mating population. The equilibrium is set by the relative disadvantages of the two homozygotes.", "mechanism_tag": "overdominance-balancing-selection", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dp_dt", "target_description": "rate of change of one allele frequency per generation under heterozygote advantage", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 150.0], "target_state": "p", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "p", "description": "frequency of the first allele", "initial_range": [0.05, 0.95]}], "expected_behaviors": ["convergence to an intermediate stable equilibrium", "approach from either side of equilibrium"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["fixation or loss of either allele", "divergence"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a subdivided population, alleles are distributed unevenly among local demes, reducing overall heterozygosity relative to a panmictic expectation. A structure index quantifies the reduction in heterozygosity caused by variance in allele frequency among demes. Researchers sample populations with differing among-deme frequency variance and mean allele frequency to record the structure index. Mating within each deme is random.", "mechanism_tag": "population-structure-fixation-index", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "F_index", "target_description": "index measuring reduction in heterozygosity due to subdivision", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "v", "description": "variance in allele frequency among demes", "range": [0.0, 0.24]}, {"symbol": "p", "description": "mean allele frequency across demes", "range": [0.1, 0.9]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["index increases with among-deme variance", "index bounded between zero and one"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["index above one", "negative index"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A quantitative trait is influenced by many additive genetic loci plus environmental variation, and offspring resemble their parents according to the additive genetic contribution. The expected shift in the offspring mean after selecting parents depends on the proportion of trait variance that is heritable and the strength of the applied selection. Breeders record the response to selection across trials with varying heritability and selection differential. Dominance and epistasis are treated as absent.", "mechanism_tag": "quantitative-selection-response", "functional_family": "polynomial", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "R", "target_description": "response to selection as the shift in offspring trait mean", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "h2", "description": "narrow-sense heritability of the trait", "range": [0.05, 0.9]}, {"symbol": "S", "description": "selection differential applied to parents", "range": [0.5, 5.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["response proportional to selection differential", "response scales with heritability"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["response exceeding selection differential when heritability is one is fine but never beyond it", "negative response for positive inputs"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A newly arisen beneficial mutation appears as a single copy in a large population and may either be lost by chance early on or survive to spread. The probability that such a mutation escapes initial stochastic loss depends on the selective advantage it confers. Researchers estimate this establishment probability across mutations with different selection coefficients. The population is large enough that later dynamics are effectively deterministic.", "mechanism_tag": "fixation-probability-new-mutation", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "P_fix", "target_description": "probability that a single-copy beneficial mutation escapes early loss", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "s", "description": "selective advantage of the mutation", "range": [0.001, 0.3]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["probability increases with selective advantage", "probability approaches a low ceiling as advantage grows", "probability near zero for negligible advantage"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["probability above one", "negative probability"]}, "id": "m2_genetics_and_population_genetics_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "genetics_and_population_genetics", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A researcher studies how arterial blood pressure recovers toward its baseline set point after a brief perturbation caused by a postural change. The baroreflex adjusts heart rate and vascular tone to drive pressure back to the regulated value. The recovery reflects a control system that senses deviation and reduces it over time. We track the deviation of mean arterial pressure from its set point as it relaxes.", "mechanism_tag": "baroreflex-pressure-relaxation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dP_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the deviation of mean arterial pressure from its regulated set point", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 60.0], "target_state": "P", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "P", "description": "Deviation of mean arterial pressure from its regulated set point", "initial_range": [10.0, 40.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decay toward zero deviation", "faster initial correction"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["growth without bound", "sustained oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_000", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "In a study of thermoregulation, a homeotherm's core body temperature is elevated by exercise and then returns toward its regulated value once activity stops. Heat loss through skin blood flow and evaporative cooling scales with the temperature excess, while metabolic heat input holds steady. The regulated recovery combines a decaying excess with a small overshoot before settling. We follow both the core temperature excess and the effective heat-dissipation state.", "mechanism_tag": "thermoregulatory-recovery", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dT_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of the core body temperature excess above set point", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 120.0], "target_state": "T", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "T", "description": "Core body temperature excess above the regulated set point", "initial_range": [0.5, 2.5]}, {"symbol": "H", "description": "Effective heat-dissipation drive from skin perfusion and evaporation", "initial_range": [0.0, 1.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["damped return toward set point", "possible slight undershoot before settling"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["runaway heating", "undamped oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_001", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A physiologist examines how oxygen consumption rate of resting animals scales across species of very different body mass. Larger organisms consume more oxygen in total but less per unit mass, reflecting integrated metabolic organization. The measurement is taken under standardized resting conditions. We seek the resting whole-organism metabolic rate as a function of body mass.", "mechanism_tag": "metabolic-mass-scaling", "functional_family": "power_law", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "Q", "target_description": "Resting whole-organism oxygen consumption rate", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "M", "description": "Body mass of the organism", "range": [0.01, 500.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic increase with mass", "sublinear scaling"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["decrease with mass", "negative rate"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_002", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A study of renal glucose handling measures the rate at which the kidney reabsorbs glucose from filtrate as plasma glucose concentration rises. Transport is carrier-mediated, so reabsorption climbs steeply at low concentrations and then plateaus as transporters saturate. Conditions are held at steady filtration. We measure tubular glucose reabsorption rate against plasma glucose concentration.", "mechanism_tag": "tubular-glucose-reabsorption", "functional_family": "saturation", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "R", "target_description": "Tubular glucose reabsorption rate", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "C", "description": "Plasma glucose concentration", "range": [0.0, 30.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["rises then plateaus at transport maximum", "monotonic increase"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["unbounded linear growth", "decrease at high concentration"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_003", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A model of glucose-insulin regulation tracks how a bolus of glucose is cleared while insulin secretion responds to elevated glucose. Glucose falls as insulin promotes uptake, and insulin itself is produced in proportion to glucose excess and degraded over time. The coupled feedback produces a transient rise in insulin and a return of glucose toward baseline. We follow plasma glucose and plasma insulin concentrations.", "mechanism_tag": "glucose-insulin-feedback", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dG_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of plasma glucose concentration above baseline", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 180.0], "target_state": "G", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "G", "description": "Plasma glucose concentration above baseline", "initial_range": [5.0, 15.0]}, {"symbol": "I", "description": "Plasma insulin concentration above baseline", "initial_range": [0.0, 2.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["glucose returns toward baseline", "transient insulin peak"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["glucose diverging upward", "negative concentrations persisting"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_004", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A population of pacemaker cells generates a membrane potential that spontaneously depolarizes, fires, and resets in a repeating cycle. A slow recovery variable interacts with a fast voltage variable to produce self-sustained rhythmic firing. No external drive is applied beyond a constant background. We track the membrane voltage and its slow recovery variable.", "mechanism_tag": "pacemaker-rhythmogenesis", "functional_family": "oscillatory", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dV_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of pacemaker membrane potential", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 50.0], "target_state": "V", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "V", "description": "Pacemaker cell membrane potential", "initial_range": [-1.5, 1.5]}, {"symbol": "w", "description": "Slow recovery variable governing repolarization", "initial_range": [-0.5, 0.5]}], "expected_behaviors": ["self-sustained limit-cycle oscillation", "repeating spike-and-reset pattern"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["decay to a fixed resting state", "unbounded voltage growth"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_005", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A pharmacophysiology experiment measures how a drug is eliminated from plasma when clearance pathways are far from saturation. After a single dose, the concentration declines steadily as the body removes a fixed fraction per unit time. Distribution is treated as instantaneous within one compartment. We follow the plasma drug concentration over time.", "mechanism_tag": "single-compartment-elimination", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dC_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of plasma drug concentration", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 24.0], "target_state": "C", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "C", "description": "Plasma drug concentration", "initial_range": [5.0, 50.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic decline toward zero", "constant fractional elimination"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["increase over time", "plateau at nonzero level"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_006", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A study of muscle oxygen delivery relates the fractional saturation of the oxygen-carrying pigment to the surrounding oxygen partial pressure. Cooperative binding among the pigment's sites produces a characteristic S-shaped uptake curve. Temperature and pH are held fixed. We measure fractional saturation as a function of oxygen partial pressure.", "mechanism_tag": "cooperative-oxygen-binding", "functional_family": "logistic", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "S", "target_description": "Fractional saturation of the oxygen-carrying pigment", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "P", "description": "Oxygen partial pressure", "range": [0.0, 120.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["sigmoidal rise from zero toward saturation", "steep intermediate region"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["exceeding full saturation", "linear response throughout"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_007", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A respiratory control study measures steady-state pulmonary ventilation as a function of arterial carbon dioxide level once chemoreceptor feedback has stabilized. Below a threshold the ventilatory drive is minimal, while above it ventilation rises approximately linearly with the stimulus. Oxygen level is held constant. We measure ventilation rate against arterial carbon dioxide partial pressure.", "mechanism_tag": "chemoreceptor-ventilatory-drive", "functional_family": "piecewise", "spec": {"model_family": "static", "target_symbol": "VE", "target_description": "Steady-state pulmonary ventilation rate", "input_symbols": [{"symbol": "Pco2", "description": "Arterial carbon dioxide partial pressure", "range": [20.0, 70.0]}], "time_symbol": null, "time_range": null, "target_state": null, "required_dynamic_states": [], "expected_behaviors": ["low ventilation below threshold", "linear rise above threshold"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["negative ventilation", "decrease with rising carbon dioxide"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_008", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
{"scenario_text": "A study of body fluid regulation examines how extracellular fluid volume returns toward its regulated value after a saline load. Excretion by the kidney rises with volume excess through pressure and hormonal feedback, while intake is held constant. The volume excess is corrected gradually as excretion exceeds intake. We follow the extracellular fluid volume excess over time.", "mechanism_tag": "fluid-volume-regulation", "functional_family": "exponential", "spec": {"model_family": "ode", "target_symbol": "dVol_dt", "target_description": "Rate of change of extracellular fluid volume excess above set point", "input_symbols": [], "time_symbol": "t", "time_range": [0.0, 48.0], "target_state": "Vol", "required_dynamic_states": [{"symbol": "Vol", "description": "Extracellular fluid volume excess above the regulated set point", "initial_range": [0.5, 3.0]}], "expected_behaviors": ["monotonic return toward set point", "excretion driven correction"], "forbidden_behaviors": ["volume diverging upward", "sustained oscillation"]}, "id": "m2_physiology_and_homeostasis_0_009", "discipline": "biology", "subfield": "physiology_and_homeostasis", "generation_mode": "M2"}
